      "reasoning": "The hand-derived cases join an ASCII lowercase province code to an uppercase country and a province code with a trailing space; both differences sit on the province side. Here the case pairs are Latin-1 (ü/Ü) and Greek (σ/Σ), which CI_AS folds through its Unicode case tables, and the country row itself is lowercase with a trailing space ('ca '), so the rtrim and the case-insensitive comparison must apply to the country side too. A conversion that lower()ed only ASCII, folded with a byte-wise or C-collation upper(), or trimmed only the province side would drop rows that SQL Server keeps. These are valid source rows: CountryRegion keys 'ÜS', 'ΣA' and 'ca ' are distinct under the case-insensitive key, and each province code references its country under that collation.",
